Description: Honour X11 window requests and publish frame extents, like i3
 X11 programs expect the window manager to act on a standard set of
 requests. The i3 session honours them; under sway's Xwayland they were
 dropped: Wine dialogs and programs restoring a saved position stayed
 put (or jumped back to the centre when they resized), wmctrl -e, -c and
 -b add,sticky did nothing, and Java never learned the frame size.
 .
 Floating X11 windows now move and resize on configure requests and on
 _NET_MOVERESIZE_WINDOW (added to _NET_SUPPORTED), keeping the fields a
 request leaves out; a position on another output moves the window to
 that output's visible workspace, a position on no output voids the
 request, and tiled or fullscreen windows keep their geometry. A window
 that floats when it's mapped appears where it asked (or where its
 WM_NORMAL_HINTS say); position (0, 0) centres its frame over its
 WM_CLIENT_LEADER window or on the workspace, like move position center.
 _NET_CLOSE_WINDOW closes the window like kill. _NET_WM_STATE_STICKY
 requests act like the sticky command, and _NET_WM_STATE reports
 whether sway considers the window sticky. _NET_FRAME_EXTENTS describes
 the window's own border and title bar, and _NET_REQUEST_FRAME_EXTENTS
 gets an estimate before mapping.
 .
 Root client messages reach sway through wlroots' XWM user event
 handler, so no extra X connection is opened. The patch is the commit
 sent upstream; it applies to 1.11 unchanged apart from context.
